<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	Privacy manifest. Every constant below is copied verbatim from the
	"Possible Values" lists of NSPrivacyCollectedDataType,
	NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes, NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType and
	NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons — not written from memory.

	WHY that matters: Apple states Xcode "won't generate a privacy report
	correctly if you define your own collected data types ... or provide your
	own reasons". An invented constant does not error — the entry is silently
	skipped, so the file looks fine and the declaration does not exist. The only
	proof it parsed is Product > Archive > Generate Privacy Report: an entry
	missing from that report does not exist for Apple.

	The previous version used NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurpose (singular) with
	English prose as its value, omitted the required ...TypeTracking key, and
	used three data-type spellings absent from Apple's list. None of those five
	entries could have parsed.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
		No tracking: nothing here is shared with a data broker or used to link
		this user to third-party data. Every entry below therefore carries
		...TypeTracking = false, and the domains array stays empty.
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>

	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<!--
			Email — Supabase Auth sign-up/sign-in (Models/AuthService.swift).
			Keyed to the account, so Linked = true.
			Was spelled ...TypeEmail, which is not an Apple constant.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeEmailAddress</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
			Name — the profile display name (Repositories/DisplayNameRepository.swift,
			stored on profiles.id) and the .fullName scope requested by Sign in
			with Apple (Features/Auth/NativeAuthView.swift:459).
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeName</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
			Physical address — the "Go home" feature stores the user's HOME
			address plus its coordinates on their own profile row
			(Repositories/HomeAddressRepository.swift writes home_address,
			home_lat, home_lng over an authenticated session). Apple's
			definition of this type names "home address" explicitly.
			This was missing entirely from the previous manifest.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePhysicalAddress</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
			Precise location. There is no bare ...TypeLocation constant; the
			choice is Precise vs Coarse, and Apple's line is "three or more
			decimal places" (~111 m).
			WHY precise: check-in asks for a fix accepted at <= 100 m
			(LocationPolicy.checkIn, Core/Services/LocationService.swift:71,
			desiredAccuracy = kCLLocationAccuracyHundredMeters) and the app never
			rounds the CLLocationCoordinate2D it receives. Full-resolution lat/lng
			also leaves the device — PlanView.swift:657 puts it in the concierge
			body — and that same request carries the chat history, whose greeting
			includes the display name (PlanView.swift:470), hence Linked = true.
			ProductPersonalization is declared alongside AppFunctionality because
			the coordinates rank the venues the user is shown.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePreciseLocation</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeProductPersonalization</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
			Payment info. Apple exempts this ONLY when the payment details are
			entered outside the app AND the developer never has access. BarPass
			fails the first half: CardPaymentView collects the card inside the
			app and tokenizes it client-side, so the exemption does not apply
			and the type stays declared.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePaymentInfo</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
			Purchase history — orders and passes are stored per account and read
			back in Profile (Core/Services/OrderHistoryService.swift,
			Features/Profile/OrderHistoryView.swift). Missing before.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurchaseHistory</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
			User ID — the Supabase account id (session.user.id) is the key every
			repository writes and filters by. Missing before.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
			Photos or videos. Note the spelling: "PhotosorVideos", with a
			lowercase "or" — that is how Apple lists it.
			Covers the profile photo AND the stories people post from inside a
			venue (Features/Tonight/VenueMediaUploader.swift → venue_media).
			Linked = TRUE, and the tempting mistake is to argue "not linked,
			the client cannot read venue_media.user_id". The label describes
			what we COLLECT and store, not what clients may query: user_id is a
			foreign key to profiles(id), so the media is linked to the person —
			and that link is the only reason we can act on a report and remove
			the poster. Declaring it not-linked would be a false declaration.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePhotosorVideos</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
			Other user content — free text the user writes that is stored with
			their id: the reason attached to a content report
			(Repositories/MediaReportRepository.swift). Same reasoning as above
			for Linked = true; the reporter's identity is what makes
			unique(media_id, reporter_id) and rate-limiting possible.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUserContent</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
			Date of birth for the 21+ age gate
			(Repositories/BirthdateRepository.swift). Apple's list has no
			"date of birth" type, and DOB is not in their definition of
			Sensitive Info (that one is race, sexual orientation, pregnancy,
			disability, religion, union membership, politics, genetics,
			biometrics), so OtherDataTypes — "any other data types not
			mentioned" — is where it belongs.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherDataTypes</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<!--
		Required reason APIs. The value is a CATEGORY (NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategory…),
		not a Type — the old file said ...APITypeFileTimestamp / ...APITypeUserDefaults,
		neither of which is a real constant. Apple lists exactly five categories:
		FileTimestamp, SystemBootTime, DiskSpace, ActiveKeyboards, UserDefaults.

		Checked against the code before deciding what stays (grep over *.swift),
		all 0 hits, so none of the three is declared: SystemBootTime
		(systemUptime, mach_absolute_time); DiskSpace (volumeAvailableCapacity*,
		systemFreeSize, systemSize, statfs/statvfs/fstatfs/fstatvfs);
		ActiveKeyboards (activeInputModes).
	-->
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<!--
			FileTimestamp. Repositories/VenueMediaRepository.swift:107 calls
			FileManager.attributesOfItem(atPath:) to read the byte size of the
			upload before starting the resumable PUT.
			C617.1 is the right reason and not a guess: it covers "the timestamps,
			size, or other metadata of files inside the app container", and the
			file is one the app itself just wrote into
			FileManager.default.temporaryDirectory (VenueMediaUploader.swift:83,
			:147, :166). Specifically NOT 3B52.1 — nothing here comes through a
			document picker; PhotosPicker hands over data the app copies into its
			own container first.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryFileTimestamp</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>C617.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
			UserDefaults — two reasons, because the app genuinely does two things:
			  CA92.1 → UserDefaults.standard, read/written only by this app
			           (24 files: AgeGateService, ThemeService, FavoritesStore,
			           LocalizationService, PointsEngine, …).
			  1C8F.1 → UserDefaults(suiteName: "group.com.sebastian.barpass")
			           in Features/GoHome/GoHomeButton.swift:19, shared with the
			           BarPassWidget extension. The App Group is real: it is in
			           both Resources/BarPass.entitlements and
			           BarPassWidget/BarPassWidget.entitlements.
			CA92.1 alone would not describe the App Group write — that is exactly
			what 1C8F.1 exists for.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
				<string>1C8F.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<!--
		DELETED: the old NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeBundle / 85F4.1 entry. The code
		was checked before removing it, not just the schema: (1) there is no
		"Bundle" category among Apple's five; (2) 85F4.1 is a DISK SPACE reason,
		and the app calls no disk-space API (grep above, 0 hits) and shows no
		disk figure to anyone — so there is nothing to re-file it under. Deleted,
		not moved.
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
